  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Any scattered showers will end this evening, with \nsome patchy fog overnight across the higher terrain of the Southern \nTier. \n\nA weak east-west oriented frontal zone will remain stalled across \nnorthern PA through this evening, with associated frontogenesis and \nmoisture convergence supporting a wealth of cloud cover across the \nregion. A few widely scattered showers will continue through early \nevening, mainly across the Southern Tier, Finger Lakes, and possibly \nthe Saint Lawrence Valley. \n\nAny remaining scattered showers will end this evening, leaving dry \nweather to prevail overnight as the frontal zone begins to move \nsoutheast in response to flow adjustments as a trough digs across \nOntario and Quebec. Clouds will persist through the first half of \nthe night before gradual north to south partial clearing late. Low \nstratus may intersect the higher terrain of the Southern Tier and \nFinger Lakes at times from late evening through early Friday \nmorning, with some hilltop fog.\n \nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather will prevail Friday through Saturday.\n\nSurface high pressure will build from northwest Ontario Friday to \nQuebec Saturday, with associated dry air and subsidence bringing a \nstretch of dry weather to the region. The timing of the next system \nhas slowed by about 6 hours compared to guidance from yesterday, so \nmost areas are now forecast to remain dry through Saturday evening.\n\nIt will be quite cool Friday night, especially across the North \nCountry where lows will drop back into the upper 30s to lower 40s. \nSome patchy frost cant be ruled out across the Tug Hill region and \nwestern foothills of the Adirondacks. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 3...A chance of showers will return Saturday night \nthrough Sunday, followed by a cooler pattern next week.\n\nThe persistent east-west frontal zone stretching from the Midwest to \nthe east coast will become more active again Saturday night through \nSunday as moisture convergence and frontogenesis increase along the \nboundary. A frontal wave will move east across Ohio and PA late \nSaturday night through Sunday, forcing the frontal zone to \ntemporarily move northward towards the NY/PA border. Increasing \nmoisture transport and isentropic upglide along and north of the \nboundary will bring increasing chances of rain late Saturday night \nthrough Sunday, with the highest rain chances and rainfall amounts \nforecast over the Southern Tier and Finger Lakes, rapidly \ndiminishing with northward extent towards the Canadian border. \n\nMonday through the middle of next week, deterministic models and \nensemble means have continued to trend steadily south with the \nposition of the east-west frontal zone as high pressure surface and \naloft strengthens over Quebec. The farther south frontal position \ncontinues to lower rain chances for Monday through Wednesday, and if \nthis model trend continues the first half of next week may end up \nbeing mainly dry. \n\nThe farther south frontal position will also yield cooler \ntemperatures, with highs only in the 60s for the first half of next \nweek.",
